import {lesson as L, sources as S} from './format.mjs';
export const foundations = [
L('electricity-1','What is an electron?','charge',S.charge,
'Explain where electrons are, what their charge means and how moving charge becomes current.',
'Imagine a copper wire sitting on your desk. It already contains electrons that can move through the metal. Connecting a battery changes the forces on those electrons, much like tilting a surface changes which way loose objects tend to slide. This is the first link between invisible particles and a computer’s visible behavior.',
['An atom contains a small nucleus with positively charged protons and usually neutral neutrons. Electrons occupy quantum states around the nucleus. A neutral atom has as many electrons as protons. Gaining one electron gives it net charge −e, where e = 1.602176634 × 10⁻¹⁹ coulombs.',
 'In a metal, some electrons can redistribute through the solid. A source establishes an electric field. The force on a carrier is F = qE, so a negative electron experiences force opposite to the field. Collisions with the lattice slow the average motion and transfer energy to the material.',
 'Current measures net charge crossing a surface per unit time. One ampere is one coulomb per second. By convention, current points in the direction a positive charge would move, so electron drift in a copper wire points the opposite way. In other materials, positive carriers can contribute to current.',
 'A change in the electromagnetic field propagates along a circuit much faster than individual electrons drift. This distinction explains why a lamp can respond promptly even though no one electron needs to travel from the switch to the lamp during that interval.'],
'If 2 coulombs pass a point every 4 seconds, the current magnitude is 2 ÷ 4 = 0.5 A. Try that in the lab, then reverse the carrier sign and compare the arrows. On a chip, silicon controls mobile charge so that later circuits can assign stable voltage ranges to logic states.',
'Electrons in atoms do not move along fixed planetary tracks. The moving symbols in the lab represent average charge transport in a simplified wire.',
'Charge has a sign. Electric fields act on charge. Current is the rate of charge transport.',
['Conventional current points right in a copper wire. Which way is the average electron drift?',['Right','Left','Neither direction'],1,'Electrons are negatively charged, so their average drift is opposite to conventional current.','Imagine a hypothetical positive carrier.']),
L('electricity-2','Voltage, current and resistance','ohm',S.ohm,
'Use Ohm’s law and electric power to predict what happens when you change a circuit.',
'A voltage is a difference in energy per unit charge. A power supply maintains that difference between its terminals. A closed path lets the field redistribute charge through the circuit and transfer energy to its components.',
['Voltage is measured in volts, current in amperes and resistance in ohms. For an ideal resistor with constant resistance, V = IR. Doubling voltage doubles current at fixed resistance.',
 'Components in series carry the same current. Their voltage drops add, so R_total = R₁ + R₂. Branches in parallel share the same voltage. Their currents add, so 1/R_total = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂.',
 'Power is energy per second. Multiply voltage by current to get P = VI, which becomes I²R for a resistor. A digital chip uses different circuit elements, but it also has to move energy and dissipate heat.'],
'A 6 V source drives 20 Ω and 10 Ω in series. The current is 0.2 A, the drops are 4 V and 2 V, and the source supplies 1.2 W. Add the branch power separately to check conservation of energy.',
'A battery can maintain a voltage across an open circuit even when sustained current through the open path is zero.',
'Voltage is energy per charge. Current is charge per time. For a constant resistor, their ratio is resistance.',
['Two equal resistors in parallel are connected to an ideal voltage source. What happens to total current versus one resistor?',['It halves','It stays the same','It doubles'],2,'Both branches draw the same current that a single resistor would draw, and those currents add.','Each branch still sees the full source voltage.']),
L('semiconductors-1','Silicon, doping and junctions','diode',S.mit,
'Explain how doping creates mobile carriers and why a PN junction responds differently to opposite voltages.',
'Pure silicon is useful because the number of mobile charges is controllable. Adding a tiny concentration of specific atoms changes which carriers are easiest to move. Joining two differently doped regions makes a device with direction-dependent behavior.',
['A silicon crystal binds electrons into bonds. Thermal energy can create an electron and a hole, an empty state that behaves like a mobile positive carrier as neighboring electrons move into it. Donor impurities increase electron concentration. Acceptor impurities increase hole concentration.',
 'At a PN junction, carriers diffuse across the boundary. Diffusion leaves behind fixed ionized dopants, producing a depletion region and an electric field. At equilibrium, field-driven drift balances diffusion.',
 'Forward bias makes the P side positive relative to the N side, lowers the barrier and increases carrier injection. Reverse bias raises the barrier. A real diode has leakage, and large reverse voltage can cause breakdown.',
 'An ideal diode model relates current to exp(V/(nVₜ)) − 1. Vₜ is around 26 mV near room temperature. This is a useful local model, but series resistance and temperature matter in real hardware.'],
'A diode can steer current in a power rectifier. In the lab, compare the magnitude of current at −0.5 V and +0.5 V with the same saturation current.',
'Doping does not normally give the whole piece of silicon a net charge. Mobile carriers and fixed dopant ions balance in the bulk.',
'Doping changes mobile carrier populations. Depletion fields arise at junctions. Bias changes the injection barrier.',
['What does forward bias do to a PN junction barrier?',['It lowers it','It raises it','It leaves it fixed'],0,'Forward bias opposes the built-in field and increases injection across the junction.','Which bias makes it easier for carriers to cross?']),
L('semiconductors-2','MOSFETs and CMOS','cmos',S.mit,
'Trace how a MOSFET gate controls a channel and how two complementary devices implement an inverter.',
'A transistor can let one electrical signal control another. A MOSFET does that with an electric field at its gate, which changes conductivity under the gate. A CMOS inverter connects complementary devices so its output tends toward the opposite rail from its input.',
['A gate conductor is separated from the semiconductor by an insulator. The gate voltage changes the electric field and carrier concentration near the surface. In enhancement-mode nMOS, sufficient positive gate-to-source voltage can form a conducting channel.',
 'An inverter connects a pMOS device to the high rail and an nMOS device to ground. Input low lets pMOS pull output high. Input high lets nMOS pull output low. Intermediate voltages can partially turn both devices on.',
 'The output node has capacitance. Changing its voltage requires charging or discharging it. Dynamic switching power scales approximately with switching activity × capacitance × voltage² × frequency. Leakage still consumes power when a gate is idle.'],
'Toggle the input and identify which transistor pulls the output. Then double supply voltage at fixed activity and capacitance in the lab model. Power rises by a factor of four. This helps explain why voltage matters when scaling processors.',
'The MOSFET gate is insulated, but changing its voltage still requires moving charge into and out of the gate capacitance.',
'A MOSFET controls conductivity with a field. In static CMOS the pull-up and pull-down networks establish the output.',
['Which transistor pulls a CMOS inverter output low when its input is high?',['pMOS','nMOS','Both equally'],1,'The nMOS forms a conductive path to ground, while the pMOS high-side path turns off in the ideal switch model.','Look for the device connected to ground.']),
L('digital-1','Logic gates and Boolean algebra','logic',S.nand,
'Predict gate outputs and construct an OR gate from NAND gates.',
'A gate takes voltage ranges interpreted as bits and produces a new bit. Boolean algebra lets you reason about those bit values without solving the transistor circuit every time.',
['AND is high only when both inputs are high. OR is high when at least one input is high. XOR is high when the inputs differ. NAND inverts AND.',
 'A truth table enumerates the outputs for every combination of inputs. Compare all rows to prove two small combinational circuits equivalent. Larger designs use symbolic methods and formal verification.',
 'NAND can invert x by feeding x into both inputs. Combining two such inversions with a third NAND gives NAND(¬A, ¬B) = A ∨ B, an instance of De Morgan’s law.',
 'Real gates have propagation delay and a limited ability to drive other inputs. Two designs with the same truth table can differ in speed, area and power.'],
'Build an OR in the circuit lab by choosing the NAND network and toggling both input bits. Check the case 00 before trusting the construction. Computers use the same gate-level operations in address decoders, comparators and arithmetic.',
'A logical high describes an acceptable voltage range for a particular circuit family. The numeric value 1 is an abstraction and does not specify a voltage.',
'Truth tables specify combinational logic. NAND is functionally complete. Delays remain even when the Boolean answer is simple.',
['Which input gives a different result for OR and XOR?',['00','01','10','11'],3,'Both are high if exactly one input is high. When both are high, OR stays high and XOR goes low.','XOR tests whether the inputs differ.']),
L('digital-2','Adders and multiplexers','alu',S.nand,
'Use a carry bit to chain additions and use a selector to choose between outputs.',
'A one-bit adder is a tiny decision circuit. It says what the current binary digit is and what carries to the next digit. Repeat that circuit and numbers of many bits become possible.',
['A half adder outputs A XOR B for sum and A AND B for carry. A full adder has a third input, carry-in. Its sum is A XOR B XOR C_in.',
 'A full adder generates a carry when at least two inputs are high. This can be written (A AND B) OR (C_in AND (A XOR B)). In a ripple adder, each stage waits for the previous carry.',
 'A two-way multiplexer chooses A when select S is 0 and B when S is 1. One gate-level form is (¬S AND A) OR (S AND B). ALUs use selection logic to choose which computed result should leave the unit.'],
'In 4-bit arithmetic, 0111 + 0001 produces 1000. The carry propagates through three lower bits. Unsigned 7 + 1 fits. Signed 7 + 1 overflows because a signed four-bit value tops out at 7.',
'Carry-out and signed overflow answer different questions. Carry-out is about unsigned range. Signed overflow depends on the operands’ signs and the result.',
'XOR produces each sum digit, majority gives the carry, and selection logic chooses an output.',
['For 4-bit addition 1111 + 0001, what is the unsigned result including carry?',['Carry 0, result 0000','Carry 1, result 0000','Carry 1, result 1111'],1,'15 + 1 is 16. The low four bits are zero and bit four records the carry.','Separate the low four bits from the fifth bit.']),
L('representation-1','Binary, hex and signed integers','bits',S.berkeley,
'Convert among binary, hexadecimal and unsigned or signed integer interpretations.',
'Bits are marks. Their meaning comes from rules agreed on by both the writer and the reader. The same eight bits can mean 255, −1, a mask, part of a color or a byte of machine code.',
['Unsigned binary assigns weights 1, 2, 4, 8 and so on from right to left. Add the weights where the bit is 1. Hex groups four bits into one digit from 0 to F.',
 'An n-bit unsigned word ranges from 0 to 2ⁿ − 1. In two’s complement the highest bit contributes −2ⁿ⁻¹, and the other weights stay positive. For eight bits the signed range is −128 to 127.',
 'To negate within a fixed width, invert all bits, then add one and discard any carry beyond the width. The most negative signed value has no representable positive counterpart at the same width.',
 'Arithmetic on fixed-width hardware can yield a truncated bit pattern. A language may assign wrapping, checked, saturating or undefined behavior depending on operation and type. Check the language rules before relying on what the machine happens to do.'],
'Hex FF groups into 1111 1111. The unsigned interpretation is 255. The signed interpretation is −128 + 127 = −1. As a negative number is widened, sign-extending with 1 preserves that value.',
'The leading bit has negative weight in two’s complement. It does not act as a standalone plus/minus flag applied to the remaining bits.',
'Width sets range. Group by four for hex. The same bits can be read in multiple ways.',
['What does 10000000 represent as an eight-bit two’s-complement value?',['0','−128','−127','128'],1,'The high bit contributes −128 and every lower bit contributes zero.','Give the leftmost bit its signed weight.']),
L('representation-2','Floating-point representation','float',S.berkeley,
'Explain how IEEE 754 binary32 encodes magnitude, precision and special values.',
'Floating point moves the binary point so you can store both very small and very large numbers. You get a limited number of significant bits. As the scale grows, the distance between adjacent representable values grows too.',
['Binary32 has one sign bit, eight exponent bits and 23 fraction bits. For a normal finite value, decode it as (−1)^sign × (1 + fraction/2²³) × 2^(exponent−127). The leading 1 is implicit.',
 'Exponent zero represents signed zero or subnormal values. Subnormals use leading 0 and exponent −126. Exponent 255 denotes infinities if fraction is zero and NaNs otherwise.',
 'Most finite decimal fractions are not exact binary fractions. The nearest binary32 value to 0.1 is approximately 0.10000000149. Rounding can also depend on intermediate precision and the rounding mode.',
 'Spacing between normal adjacent values in [1, 2) is 2⁻²³. It doubles in [2, 4). This matters for numerical stability in graphics, physics and model training.'],
'Enter 0.1 and 0.5 in the lab. Observe the rounded result and raw bits. Since 0.5 = 2⁻¹, its significand is exactly 1.0 and exponent field is 126.',
'A NaN is a designated encoding, and unequal comparisons involving one need special handling. Check for it explicitly when diagnosing numerical routines.',
'Exponent controls scale, fraction controls relative precision, and reserved exponents cover special cases.',
['What is the binary32 biased exponent field for 1.0?',['0','126','127','255'],2,'1.0 is 1.0 × 2⁰, so the stored exponent is 0 + 127.','Use the normal encoding’s bias.']),
L('architecture-1','The instruction cycle','cpu',S.berkeley,
'Trace fetch, decode and execute, identifying the observable state after each instruction.',
'Memory holds instruction encodings. The processor reads one at the address in the program counter, interprets it and updates state. A program is an organized sequence of these changes.',
['Fetch reads bytes at the program counter. On a simple fixed-width ISA, adding the instruction length gives the default next address.',
 'Decode splits a bit pattern into fields such as opcode, destination, inputs and immediate. An ISA specifies which fields mean what. Control logic chooses the appropriate datapath operations.',
 'Execute may run arithmetic in the ALU, branch using a comparison, or compute a memory address. Memory instructions read or write data. Register writeback makes a computed value available to later instructions.',
 'Real processors can overlap these stages and run operations out of order internally. The architectural state visible to software has to follow the ISA’s rules.'],
'In the teaching CPU, step through MOV R0, 3, ADD R0, 2 and STORE R0, 0. Predict the register immediately after ADD and the memory cell after STORE. This lab groups execution and writeback in one step.',
'The program counter identifies instructions. Data operands are read from registers or addressed data memory as specified by the instruction.',
'Fetch a pattern, decode its fields, perform the defined operation and update architectural state.',
['Which instruction in the example first changes data memory?',['MOV','ADD','STORE'],2,'The first two instructions update R0. STORE copies R0 to an addressed data cell.','Track destinations, not just values.']),
L('assembly-1','Instructions and addressing','cpu',S.risc,
'Read a short load/store program and relate assembly operands to addresses and values.',
'Assembly exposes the machine’s storage locations and operations. In a load/store ISA such as base RISC-V, most arithmetic uses registers. Explicit instructions move data between registers and memory.',
['A register name refers to a small, fast architectural storage location. An immediate is a literal encoded inside an instruction, within the range supported by its format.',
 'In RISC-V, an instruction such as lw a0, 8(sp) computes the byte address sp + 8, loads a 32-bit word and writes the destination register. On RV64, lw sign-extends to the register width. Endianness controls how the bytes of the word are ordered.',
 'A store such as sw a0, 8(sp) computes the address in the same way and writes the low 32 bits of a0. The base register sp does not change just because the instruction uses it as an address.',
 'Assembler mnemonics can be pseudoinstructions. For example, a large load-immediate operation may expand into more than one actual instruction. ABI register names add calling roles on top of register numbers.'],
'If sp is 0x1000, the word address for lw a0, 8(sp) is 0x1008. In the educational lab, edit the smaller MOV, ADD and STORE program to write 7 to cell 2. The toy machine uses cell addresses and its own instruction format.',
'An address identifies a place. The value stored at that place may be numerically unrelated to the address.',
'An ISA defines instruction behavior. Loads and stores use effective addresses. The ABI establishes conventions across code.',
['If sp = 0x2000, what address does sw a0, 12(sp) write?',['0x2000','0x2012','0x200C','Whatever value is in a0'],2,'The literal 12 is decimal, equivalent to hexadecimal C. The address is base plus displacement.','Convert 12 to hex before adding.']),
L('memory-1','Cache and locality','cache',S.berkeley,
'Calculate the line, index and tag for a direct-mapped cache access.',
'A cache keeps recently useful blocks close to the processor. An access can pay for moving several adjacent bytes even if the program asked for just one. That often helps because programs tend to access nearby locations.',
['Temporal locality is reuse over time. Spatial locality is access to nearby addresses. A line is the aligned memory block transferred as a unit.',
 'For line size B bytes, floor(address/B) gives the block number. In a direct-mapped cache with N lines, block mod N gives the index and floor(block/N) gives the tag. An access hits only if the selected line is valid and the tag matches.',
 'Two different blocks mapped to the same index evict each other. Set associativity allows several blocks at one index. Real systems also track writes, manage replacement and maintain coherence across cores.',
 'An approximate mean access time for a single-level model is hit time + miss rate × extra miss penalty. Measuring the miss rate alone misses the impact of different penalties.'],
'With four lines and four bytes per line, addresses 0 and 3 share block 0 and index 0. Address 16 is block 4, also index 0, with a different tag. Try the access sequence 0, 3, 16, 0. The final access misses.',
'Two nearby addresses can fall in different blocks if a line boundary separates them.',
'Divide by line size for the block, use modulo for the index and compare the tag to decide whether it hits.',
['A four-line direct-mapped cache has four-byte lines. Which address conflicts with address 0?',['3','4','12','16'],3,'Block 4 shares index 0 with block 0, but its tag differs.','Use the block number, then take modulo 4.']),
L('modern-cpu-1','Pipelines and branch prediction','pipeline',S.berkeley,
'Explain how overlapping stages improves throughput and why branch mistakes waste work.',
'Imagine an assembly line with a station for each part of a task. Each item still visits every station. Once the line fills, different items can occupy different stations during the same clock cycle.',
['A simple teaching pipeline separates fetch, decode, execute, memory and writeback. In an ideal path, one instruction can retire per cycle after the initial fill.',
 'A data hazard occurs when an instruction needs a result still in flight. Forwarding routes results directly to a later instruction where timing allows. A load-use dependency can still require a stall.',
 'A branch creates uncertainty about which instruction to fetch. A predictor guesses the direction before execution resolves the condition. On a wrong prediction, younger wrong-path work is discarded and fetch resumes at the correct location.',
 'A one-bit predictor remembers the last outcome. A two-bit saturating counter needs stronger evidence to change its prediction, making occasional exceptions less disruptive. Actual CPUs combine several kinds of history.'],
'Run T T T N T T T N in the predictor lab and compare one-bit and two-bit counters. Count which transitions cause extra misses. The lab models prediction and a fixed penalty rather than an entire pipeline.',
'Pipeline throughput can improve without reducing the time for one instruction. A branch penalty depends on where the outcome becomes known and how the engine recovers.',
'Pipelining overlaps work. Hazards limit that overlap. Predictors keep the front end supplied with likely instructions.',
['What must happen to younger wrong-path instructions after a branch mispredicts?',['They must update architectural state anyway','They must be discarded before architectural effects commit','The branch instruction must be ignored'],1,'Executing wrong-path work can be safe only if its architectural effects never commit.','Think about the architectural order a program expects.'])
];
